# Deduplicating on a value of a type that isn't hashable (Struct here) must be a
# graceful error, not a Rust panic. Value::hash() has a guard
# (`if !self.value_type().is_hashable() { panic!(...) }`) meant to be prevented by
# callers checking is_hashable() first -- sort.rs does this via is_comparable(), but
# uniq.rs had no such check before calling HashSet::contains/insert on a Row, so a
# Struct-typed column reached the panic directly. Fixed by checking each actual value's
# runtime type before hashing and returning a graceful command_error instead.
#
# A `select`-computed column is always statically declared as $any (a closure's output
# type is never known ahead of time -- see Closure::output_type), so the unhashable
# value only exists at runtime; this can't be caught by checking the column's declared
# type the way sort.rs does, only by checking each actual value as it's processed.
#
# uniq must be the *last* command in the pipe, not piped further to echo: Job::eval()
# only tracks/joins the thread for the last command of a pipeline -- a non-last stage's
# spawned thread is never joined at all, so its result (whether a panic or a properly
# propagated graceful error) would never reach the try/catch below. See test-gaps.md's
# `Job::eval()` thread-join-gap entry.
#
# The `echo "barrier"` below is deliberate, not decorative -- see the identical note in
# last_command_error.crush: touching $msg in the very next statement after the catch
# block races.
$msg := ""
$got := $false
try {
    $t := $(csv:from "a,1\nb,2\n" key=$string val=$integer)
    $t | select s={struct:of val=$val} | uniq
} catch { |$e|
    $got = $true
    $msg = $e
}
echo "barrier"

assert $got "expected the unhashable-type error to be caught, not silently swallowed"

$panicked := $($msg:like "*panicked*")
assert ($panicked == $false) "expected a graceful error message, not a Rust panic"

$len := $($msg:len)
assert ($len > 0) "expected a non-empty caught error message"
